able_fragments` \u2014 combinations: (i==0, skip=True) \u2192 fatal=True; (i>0, skip=True) \u2192 fatal=False; (i>0, skip=False) \u2192 fatal=True; (i==0, skip=False) \u2192 fatal=True.\n\nLine 44-47: `fragment.get('url')` truthy vs falsy \u2192 urljoin branch with assert fragment_base_url.\n\nLine 48-52: success True \u2192 append, break; success False \u2192 return False.\n\nLine 53-62: HTTPError with count <= retries \u2192 report_retry_fragment; HTTPError with count > retries \u2192 no report.\n\nLine 63-69: DownloadError with not fatal \u2192 skip, break; DownloadError with fatal \u2192 raise.\n\nLine 71-76: count > retries with not fatal \u2192 skip, continue; with fatal \u2192 report_error, return False. Also count <= retries (success) \u2192 proceed.\n\nLine 78-80: finish, return True.\n\nNow, how does ctx['fragment_index'] get set? In the real FragmentFD._prepare_frag_download:\n\nLet me recall youtube_dl/downloader/fragment.py:\n\n```python\n    def _prepare_and_start_frag_download(self, ctx):\n        self._prepare_frag_download(ctx)\n        self._start_frag_download(ctx)\n\n    def _prepare_frag_download(self, ctx):\n        if 'ctx' in self.__dict__:\n            raise Exception('Already exists')\n        ctx = self.ctx = ctx.copy()\n        ctx['fragment_index'] = 0\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, I'm not 100% sure. Let me use get_info to check `_prepare_and_start_frag_download`, `_download_fragment`, `_append_fragment`, `_finish_frag_download`, `report_retry_fragment`, `report_skip_fragment`, `report_error`, and the classes.\n\nActually, for the resume test (line 36-37 continue branch), I need ctx['fragment_index'] > 0 after `_prepare_and_start_frag_download`. If the real implementation sets it to 0 always, then to test the continue branch I'd need to mock `_prepare_and_start_frag_download` to set fragment_index to e.g. 1, with 2 fragments. Then frag 1 is skipped (continue), frag 2 is downloaded.\n\nAlternatively, I mock `_prepare_and_start_frag_download` in all tests to keep things simple and deterministic. That way I control ctx['fragment_index'].\n\nBut hold on \u2014 if I mock `_prepare_and_start_frag_download`, do I still cover line 28? Yes, line 28 is the call site in dash.py; it executes regardless.\n\nHmm, but is it better to use the real methods? The real `_download_fragment` does:\n\n```python\n    def _download_fragment(self, ctx, frag_url, info_dict, headers=None, request_data=None):\n        fragment_filename = '%s-Frag%d' % (ctx['tmpfilename'], ctx['fragment_index'])\n        ...\n        try:\n            success = ctx['dl'].download(fragment_filename, {...})\n        ...\n        if not success:\n            return False, None\n        ...\n        return True, frag_content\n```\n\nThat's complex. Mocking is much simpler. I'll mock the fragment lifecycle methods but keep the real `report_retry_fragment`, `report_skip_fragment`, `report_error`? Those call `self.ydl.report_error` etc. Actually:\n\n- `report_error` in FileDownloader: `def report_error(self, message, tb=None, expected=False): self.ydl.report_error(message, tb, expected)`. Hmm, actually it might be `self.ydl.report_error(...)` which raises... YoutubeDL.report_error raises DownloadError? No wait \u2014 YoutubeDL.report_error doesn't raise; `trouble` raises. Let me check: YoutubeDL.report_error:\n\n```python\n    def report_error(self, message, tb=None, expected=False):\n        '''Do the same as trouble but return the error instead of raising it'''\n        return self.trouble(message, tb, expected)\n```\n\nHmm no. Let me check. In youtube_dl/YoutubeDL.py:\n\n```python\n    def trouble(self, message=None, tb=None, expected=False):\n        if message is not None:\n            self.to_stderr(message)\n        if not expected:\n            message = ... traceback ...\n        if self.params.get('ignoreerrors'):\n            ...\n            return\n        raise DownloadError(message, tb)\n```\n\nHmm, and report_error:\n\n```python\n    def report_error(self, message, tb=None, expected=False):\n        '''\n        Do the same as trouble, but prefixes the message with 'ERROR:'\n        '''\n        self.trouble('ERROR: %s' % message, tb, expected)\n```\n\nHmm, I'm not sure about the exact signature. In FileDownloader:\n\n```python\n    def report_error(self, message, tb=None, expected=False):\n        '''\n        Fail if the error is fatal, else print a warning\n        '''\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm no. Let me just use get_info to check these.\n\nActually, since I'm going to mock ydl anyway (MagicMock), calling real `report_error` would call `self.ydl.report_error(...)` on the mock, which is fine and lets me assert it was called. But wait \u2014 FileDownloader.report_error might do more. Let me check with get_info.\n\nLet me plan the get_info calls:\n- `DashSegmentsFD` \u2014 class info\n- `FragmentFD` \u2014 class info\n- `FragmentFD._download_fragment`\n- `FragmentFD._append_fragment`\n- `FragmentFD._finish_frag_download`\n- `FragmentFD._prepare_and_start_frag_download`\n- `FragmentFD.report_retry_fragment`\n- `FragmentFD.report_skip_fragment`\n- `FileDownloader.report_error`\n- `FileDownloader.__init__`\n- `DownloadError`\n- `urljoin`\n\nLet me do these calls. I can batch independent ones.\n\nNow let me draft the test file:\n\n```python\nimport io\n\nimport pytest\n\nfrom youtube_dl.compat import compat_urllib_error\nfrom youtube_dl.downloader.dash import DashSegmentsFD\nfrom youtube_dl.utils import DownloadError\n\n\ndef _http_error():\n    return compat_urllib_error.HTTPError(\n        'http://example.com/frag', 404, 'Not Found', {}, io.BytesIO(b''))\n\n\ndef _make_fd(params, monkeypatch, fragment_index=0, download_results=None, ...):\n    ...\n```\n\nHmm, let me think about how to structure the mocking. I'll create a helper that builds a DashSegmentsFD with mocked fragment methods, recording calls.\n\n```python\nclass FakeFragmentFD(DashSegmentsFD):\n    ...\n```\n\nSubclassing could work but then the code under test is the inherited real_download \u2014 that's fine, coverage tracks the file/line, and real_download is defined in dash.py so subclass instances still execute dash.py lines. But wait \u2014 coverage measures lines in dash.py; calling the inherited method from a subclass still executes the code in dash.py. Yes, that works.\n\nBut simpler: instantiate DashSegmentsFD and monkeypatch its instance methods.\n\n```python\ndef make_fd(monkeypatch, params, *, fragment_index=0, download_results=None):\n    ydl = MagicMock()\n    fd = DashSegmentsFD(ydl, params)\n    calls = {'prepare': [], 'download': [], 'append': [], 'finish': []}\n\n    def fake_prepare(ctx):\n        calls['prepare'].append(ctx)\n        ctx['fragment_index'] = fragment_index\n\n    def fake_download(ctx, url, info_dict):\n        calls['download'].append(url)\n        result = download_results.pop(0) if download_results else (True, b'frag')\n        if isinstance(result, Exception):\n            raise result\n        return result\n\n    def fake_append(ctx, content):\n        calls['append'].append(content)\n\n    def fake_finish(ctx):\n        calls['finish'].append(ctx)\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_prepare_and_start_frag_download', fake_prepare)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_download_fragment', fake_download)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_append_fragment', fake_append)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_finish_frag_download', fake_finish)\n    fd.calls = calls\n    return fd\n```\n\nWait, monkeypatch.setattr on an instance attribute \u2014 monkeypatch.setattr(obj, name, value) works for instance attributes too. Yes, `monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_download_fragment', fake_download)` sets the instance attribute and restores afterwards (deletes it). That's fine.\n\nHmm, but actually since these are per-test fresh objects, I could just assign directly: `fd._download_fragment = fake_download`. But the instructions say to use monkeypatch or pytest-mock if appropriate for cleanup. Since each test creates its own fd instance, there's no global state pollution. But using monkeypatch is safer/cleaner. I'll use monkeypatch.\n\nNow the tests:\n\n**Test 1: full success with explicit urls, test=False**\n\n```python\ndef test_full_download_success(monkeypatch):\n    params = {}\n    info_dict = {\n        'fragments': [{'url': 'http://example.com/seg1'}, {'url': 'http://example.com/seg2'}],\n    }\n    fd = make_fd(monkeypatch, params)\n    result = fd.real_download('file.mp4', info_dict)\n    assert result is True\n    assert fd.calls['download'] == ['http://example.com/seg1', 'http://example.com/seg2']\n    assert fd.calls['append'] == [b'frag', b'frag']\n    assert len(fd.calls['finish']) == 1\n```\n\nCovers: 19-21 (else branch), 23-28, 30-31, 33-36 (loop, no continue), 40 (fatal computed), 42-52 (success path), 78-80.\n\n**Test 2: test=True downloads only first fragment**\n\n```python\ndef test_test_mode_only_first_fragment(monkeypatch):\n    params = {'test': True}\n    info_dict = {\n        'fragments': [{'url': 'http://example.com/seg1'}, {'url': 'http://example.com/seg2'}],\n    }\n    fd = make_fd(monkeypatch, params)\n    result = fd.real_download('file.mp4', info_dict)\n    assert result is True\n    assert fd.calls['download'] == ['http://example.com/seg1']\n```\n\nCovers the `[:1]` branch of line 20-21.\n\n**Test 3: fragment without url uses fragment_base_url + path**\n\n```python\ndef test_fragment_url_from_base_and_path(monkeypatch):\n    params = {}\n    info_dict = {\n        'fragment_base_url': 'http://example.com/base/',\n        'fragments': [{'path': 'seg1.m4s'}],\n    }\n    fd = make_fd(monkeypatch, params)\n    result = fd.real_download('file.mp4', info_dict)\n    assert result is True\n    assert fd.calls['download'] == ['http://example.com/base/seg1.m4s']\n```\n\nCovers lines 45-47. urljoin('http://example.com/base/', 'seg1.m4s') \u2192 'http://example.com/base/seg1.m4s'. Let me double check urljoin behavior: `compat_urlparse.urljoin('http://example.com/base/', 'seg1.m4s')` \u2192 'http://example.com/base/seg1.m4s'. Yes.\n\nWait, what is `urljoin` in youtube_dl.utils? Let me check with get_info. It might be `compat_urlparse.urljoin`. Yes, in youtube_dl/utils.py: `urljoin = compat_urlparse.urljoin`. OK.\n\n**Test 4: _download_fragment returns False \u2192 real_download returns False**\n\n```python\ndef test_download_fragment_failure_returns_false(monkeypatch):\n    params = {}\n    info_dict = {'fragments': [{'url': 'http://example.com/seg1'}]}\n    fd = make_fd(monkeypatch, params, download_results=[(False, None)])\n    result = fd.real_download('file.mp4', info_dict)\n    assert result is False\n    assert fd.calls['append'] == []\n    assert fd.calls['finish'] == []\n```\n\nCovers lines 49-50.\n\n**Test 5: HTTPError retried then success**\n\n```python\ndef test_http_error_retried(monkeypatch):\n    params = {'fragment_retries': 2}\n    info_dict = {'fragments': [{'url': 'http://example.com/seg1'}]}\n    err = _http_error()\n    fd = make_fd(monkeypatch, params, download_results=[err, (True, b'frag')])\n    result = fd.real_download('file.mp4', info_dict)\n    assert result is True\n    assert len(fd.calls['download']) == 2\n    # report_retry_fragment called once\n```\n\nFor report_retry_fragment \u2014 should I mock it or let the real one run? The real one:\n\n```python\n    def report_retry_fragment(self, err, frag_index, retry, retries):\n        self.to_screen(...)\n```\n\nHmm, let me check. In FragmentFD:\n\n```python\n    def report_retry_fragment(self, err, frag_index, count, retries):\n        '''Report fragment retry'''\n        self.to_screen('[%s] Got error HTTP error %s: %s. Retrying fragment %d (attempt %d of %s)...'\n                       % (self.FD_NAME, err, ...))\n```\n\nSomething like that. It calls self.to_screen which calls self.ydl.to_screen. With a MagicMock ydl, that's fine. But to assert it was called, I could mock report_retry_fragment itself and assert calls. Hmm \u2014 but if I mock it, I don't exercise the real one; that's fine since it's in fragment.py, not dash.py. The coverage target is dash.py lines 19-80. So mocking report_retry_fragment and report_skip_fragment lets me assert precisely.\n\nActually wait \u2014 but do I want to assert that report_retry_fragment was called with the right args? Yes, that's a good postcondition check.\n\nLet me mock `report_retry_fragment` and `report_skip_fragment` too, recording calls. And `report_error` \u2014 the real FileDownloader.report_error calls `self.ydl.report_error` which... let me check FileDownloader.report_error:\n\n```python\n    def report_error(self, message, tb=None, expected=False):\n        '''\n        Fail if the error is fatal, else print a warning\n        '''\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, actually I recall in FileDownloader:\n\n```python\n    def report_error(self, message, tb=None, expected=False):\n        '''\n        Fail if the error is fatal, else print a warning\n        '''\n        self.ydl.report_error(message, tb, expected)\n```\n\nHmm no wait. Let me think. In youtube_dl/downloader/common.py:\n\n```python\n    def report_error(self, message, tb=None, expected=False):\n        '''\n        Fail if the error is fatal, else print a warning\n        '''\n        self.ydl.report_error(message, tb, expected)\n```\n\nHmm, I don't think that's right either. Let me just check with get_info. Actually, in youtube_dl/downloader/common.py FileDownloader:\n\n```python\n    def _hook_progress(self, status, info_dict):\n        ...\n\n    def report_error(self, message, tb=None, expected=False):\n        '''\n        Fail if the error is fatal, else print a warning\n        '''\n        self.ydl.report_error(message, tb, expected)\n```\n\nI really need to check. Let me include it in get_info calls.\n\nActually, whether I mock report_error or not: if the real report_error calls ydl.report_error (a MagicMock), nothing raises, and I can assert `fd.ydl.report_error.called`