ode under test is the inherited real_download \u2014 that's fine, coverage tracks the file/line, and real_download is defined in dash.py so subclass instances still execute dash.py lines. But wait \u2014 coverage measures lines in dash.py; calling the inherited method from a subclass still executes the code in dash.py. Yes, that works.\n\nBut simpler: instantiate DashSegmentsFD and monkeypatch its instance methods.\n\n```python\ndef make_fd(monkeypatch, params, *, fragment_index=0, download_results=None):\n    ydl = MagicMock()\n    fd = DashSegmentsFD(ydl, params)\n    calls = {'prepare': [], 'download': [], 'append': [], 'finish': []}\n\n    def fake_prepare(ctx):\n        calls['prepare'].append(ctx)\n        ctx['fragment_index'] = fragment_index\n\n    def fake_download(ctx, url, info_dict):\n        calls['download'].append(url)\n        result = download_results.pop(0) if download_results else (True, b'frag')\n        if isinstance(result, Exception):\n            raise result\n        return result\n\n    def fake_append(ctx, content):\n        calls['append'].append(content)\n\n    def fake_finish(ctx):\n        calls['finish'].append(ctx)\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_prepare_and_start_frag_download', fake_prepare)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_download_fragment', fake_download)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_append_fragment', fake_append)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_finish_frag_download', fake_finish)\n    fd.calls = calls\n    return fd\n```\n\nWait, monkeypatch.setattr on an instance attribute \u2014 monkeypatch.setattr(obj, name, value) works for instance attributes too. Yes, `monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_download_fragment', fake_download)` sets the instance attribute and restores afterwards (deletes it). That's fine.\n\nHmm, but actually since these are per-test fresh objects, I could just assign directly: `fd._download_fragment = fake_download`. But the instructions say to use monkeypatch or pytest-mock if appropriate for cleanup. Since each test creates its own fd instance, there's no global state pollution. But using monkeypatch is safer/cleaner. I'll use monkeypatch.\n\nNow the tests:\n\n**Test 1: full success with explicit urls, test=False**\n\n```python\ndef test_full_download_success(monkeypatch):\n    params = {}\n    info_dict = {\n        'fragments': [{'url': 'http://example.com/seg1'}, {'url': 'http://example.com/seg2'}],\n    }\n    fd = make_fd(monkeypatch, params)\n    result = fd.real_download('file.mp4', info_dict)\n    assert result is True\n    assert fd.calls['download'] == ['http://example.com/seg1', 'http://example.com/seg2']\n    assert fd.calls['append'] == [b'frag', b'frag']\n    assert len(fd.calls['finish']) == 1\n```\n\nCovers: 19-21 (else branch), 23-28, 30-31, 33-36 (loop, no continue), 40 (fatal computed), 42-52 (success path), 78-80.\n\n**Test 2: test=True downloads only first fragment**\n\n```python\ndef test_test_mode_only_first_fragment(monkeypatch):\n    params = {'test': True}\n    info_dict = {\n        'fragments': [{'url': 'http://example.com/seg1'}, {'url': 'http://example.com/seg2'}],\n    }\n    fd = make_fd(monkeypatch, params)\n    result = fd.real_download('file.mp4', info_dict)\n    assert result is True\n    assert fd.calls['download'] == ['http://example.com/seg1']\n```\n\nCovers the `[:1]` branch of line 20-21.\n\n**Test 3: fragment without url uses fragment_base_url + path**\n\n```python\ndef test_fragment_url_from_base_and_path(monkeypatch):\n    params = {}\n    info_dict = {\n        'fragment_base_url': 'http://example.com/base/',\n        'fragments': [{'path': 'seg1.m4s'}],\n    }\n    fd = make_fd(monkeypatch, params)\n    result = fd.real_download('file.mp4', info_dict)\n    assert result is True\n    assert fd.calls['download'] == ['http://example.com/base/seg1.m4s']\n```\n\nCovers lines 45-47. urljoin('http://example.com/base/', 'seg1.m4s') \u2192 'http://example.com/base/seg1.m4s'. Let me double check urljoin behavior: `compat_urlparse.urljoin('http://example.com/base/', 'seg1.m4s')` \u2192 'http://example.com/base/seg1.m4s'. Yes.\n\nWait, what is `urljoin` in youtube_dl.utils? Let me check with get_info. It might be `compat_urlparse.urljoin`. Yes, in youtube_dl/utils.py: `urljoin = compat_urlparse.urljoin`. OK.\n\n**Test 4: _download_fragment returns False \u2192 real_download returns False**\n\n```python\ndef test_download_fragment_failure_returns_false(monkeypatch):\n    params = {}\n    info_dict = {'fragments': [{'url': 'http://example.com/seg1'}]}\n    fd = make_fd(monkeypatch, params, download_results=[(False, None)])\n    result = fd.real_download('file.mp4', info_dict)\n    assert result is False\n    assert fd.calls['append'] == []\n    assert fd.calls['finish'] == []\n```\n\nCovers lines 49-50.\n\n**Test 5: HTTPError retried then success**\n\n```python\ndef test_http_error_retried(monkeypatch):\n    params = {'fragment_retries': 2}\n    info_dict = {'fragments': [{'url': 'http://example.com/seg1'}]}\n    err = _http_error()\n    fd = make_fd(monkeypatch, params, download_results=[err, (True, b'frag')])\n    result = fd.real_download('file.mp4', info_dict)\n    assert result is True\n    assert len(fd.calls['download']) == 2\n    # report_retry_fragment called once\n```\n\nFor report_retry_fragment \u2014 should I mock it or let the real one run? The real one:\n\n```python\n    def report_retry_fragment(self, err, frag_index, retry, retries):\n        self.to_screen(...)\n```\n\nHmm, let me check. In FragmentFD:\n\n```python\n    def report_retry_fragment(self, err, frag_index, count, retries):\n        '''Report fragment retry'''\n        self.to_screen('[%s] Got error HTTP error %s: %s. Retrying fragment %d (attempt %d of %s)...'\n                       % (self.FD_NAME, err, ...))\n```\n\nSomething like that. It calls self.to_screen which calls self.ydl.to_screen. With a MagicMock ydl, that's fine. But to assert it was called, I could mock report_retry_fragment itself and assert calls. Hmm \u2014 but if I mock it, I don't exercise the real one; that's fine since it's in fragment.py, not dash.py. The coverage target is dash.py lines 19-80. So mocking report_retry_fragment and report_skip_fragment lets me assert precisely.\n\nActually wait \u2014 but do I want to assert that report_retry_fragment was called with the right args? Yes, that's a good postcondition check.\n\nLet me mock `report_retry_fragment` and `report_skip_fragment` too, recording calls. And `report_error` \u2014 the real FileDownloader.report_error calls `self.ydl.report_error` which... let me check FileDownloader.report_error:\n\n```python\n    def report_error(self, message, tb=None, expected=False):\n        '''\n        Fail if the error is fatal, else print a warning\n        '''\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, actually I recall in FileDownloader:\n\n```python\n    def report_error(self, message, tb=None, expected=False):\n        '''\n        Fail if the error is fatal, else print a warning\n        '''\n        self.ydl.report_error(message, tb, expected)\n```\n\nHmm no wait. Let me think. In youtube_dl/downloader/common.py:\n\n```python\n    def report_error(self, message, tb=None, expected=False):\n        '''\n        Fail if the error is fatal, else print a warning\n        '''\n        self.ydl.report_error(message, tb, expected)\n```\n\nHmm, I don't think that's right either. Let me just check with get_info. Actually, in youtube_dl/downloader/common.py FileDownloader:\n\n```python\n    def _hook_progress(self, status, info_dict):\n        ...\n\n    def report_error(self, message, tb=None, expected=False):\n        '''\n        Fail if the error is fatal, else print a warning\n        '''\n        self.ydl.report_error(message, tb, expected)\n```\n\nI really need to check. Let me include it in get_info calls.\n\nActually, whether I mock report_error or not: if the real report_error calls ydl.report_error (a MagicMock), nothing raises, and I can assert `fd.ydl.report_error.called`. That works. But if FileDownloader.report_error has a different implementation (e.g., calls self.trouble which raises DownloadError), then the test would break. Safer to mock report_error on the fd instance and assert it was called. Since report_error is defined in common.py (not dash.py), mocking it doesn't reduce dash.py coverage.\n\nHmm wait, actually there's a subtlety: `self.report_error('giving up after %s fragment retries' % fragment_retries)` at line 75. If I mock it, I can assert the message. Good.\n\nSimilarly `report_skip_fragment` \u2014 in FragmentFD:\n\n```python\n    def report_skip_fragment(self, frag_index):\n        self.to_screen('[download] Skipping fragment %d...' % frag_index)\n```\n\nMock it to assert.\n\nAnd `report_retry_fragment` \u2014 mock to assert.\n\nOK here's my refined helper:\n\n```python\ndef make_fd(monkeypatch, params, *, fragment_index=0, download_results=None):\n    ydl = MagicMock()\n    fd = DashSegmentsFD(ydl, params)\n    calls = defaultdict(list)\n\n    def fake_prepare(ctx):\n        calls['prepare'].append(ctx)\n        ctx['fragment_index'] = fragment_index\n\n    def fake_download(ctx, url, info_dict):\n        calls['download'].append(url)\n        if download_results:\n            result = download_results.pop(0)\n        else:\n            result = (True, b'frag-content')\n        if isinstance(result, Exception):\n            raise result\n        return result\n\n    def fake_append(ctx, content):\n        calls['append'].append(content)\n\n    def fake_finish(ctx):\n        calls['finish'].append(ctx)\n\n    def fake_report_retry(err, frag_index, count, retries):\n        calls['retry'].append((err, frag_index, count, retries))\n\n    def fake_report_skip(frag_index):\n        calls['skip'].append(frag_index)\n\n    def fake_report_error(msg):\n        calls['error'].append(msg)\n\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_prepare_and_start_frag_download', fake_prepare)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_download_fragment', fake_download)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_append_fragment', fake_append)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_finish_frag_download', fake_finish)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, 'report_retry_fragment', fake_report_retry)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, 'report_skip_fragment', fake_report_skip)\n    monkeypatch.setattr(fd, 'report_error', fake_report_error)\n    fd.calls = calls\n    return fd\n```\n\nHmm, wait: `report_error` signature \u2014 FileDownloader.report_error(self, message, tb=None, expected=False)? If dash.py calls `self.report_error('giving up after %s fragment retries' % fragment_retries)` with one arg, my fake with one param works.\n\nNow the tests:\n\n**Test 5: HTTPError retried then success** (covers 53-62 with report)\n\n```python\ndef test_http_error_retried_then_success(monkeypatch):\n    params = {'fragment_retries': 2}\n    info_dict = {'fragments': [{'url': 'http://example.com/seg1'}]}\n    err = _http_error()\n    fd = make_fd(monkeypatch, params, download_results=[err, (True, b'frag')])\n    result = fd.real_download('file.mp4', info_dict)\n    assert result is True\n    assert len(fd.calls['download']) == 2\n    assert fd.calls['retry'] == [(err, 1, 1, 2)]\n    assert fd.calls['finish'] != []\n```\n\nWait \u2014 the retry call args: `self.report_retry_fragment(err, frag_index, count, fragment_retries)` \u2192 (err, 1, 1, 2). Yes.\n\n**Test 6: HTTPError exhausted, fatal (first fragment) \u2192 report_error, return False** (covers 71-75 fatal branch)\n\n```python\ndef test_http_error_gives_up_fatal(monkeypatch):\n    params = {'fragment_retries': 1}\n    info_dict = {'fragments': [{'url': 'http://example.com/seg1'}]}\n    err1, err2 = _http_error(), _http_error()\n    fd = make_fd(monkeypatch, params, download_results=[err1, err2])\n    result = fd.real_download('file.mp4', info_dict)\n    assert result is False\n    assert len(fd.calls['download']) == 2\n    assert fd.calls['retry'] == [(err1, 1, 1, 1)]  # second failure: count=2 > retries=1, no report\n    assert fd.calls['error'] == ['giving up after 1 fragment retries']\n    assert fd.calls['finish'] == []\n```\n\nWait let's trace: fragment_retries=1. count=0, loop: count(0) <= 1 \u2192 try \u2192 HTTPError \u2192 count=1 \u2192 1 <= 1 \u2192 report_retry(err, 1, 1, 1). Loop: count(1) <= 1 \u2192 try \u2192 HTTPError \u2192 count=2 \u2192 2 <= 1 False \u2192 no report. Loop: count(2) <= 1 False \u2192 exit. count(2) > 1 \u2192 fatal (i==0) \u2192 report_error('giving up after 1 fragment retries') \u2192 return False. \n\nSo download called twice, retry reported once with (err1, 1, 1, 1), error reported once. finish not called. \n\n**Test 7: HTTPError exhausted, not fatal (later fragment, skip_unavailable=True) \u2192 skip, continue** (covers 71-74 not-fatal branch)\n\n```python\ndef test_http_error_gives_up_nonfatal_skips(monkeypatch):\n    params = {'fragment_retries': 0}\n    info_dict = {'fragments': [\n        {'url': 'http://example.com/seg1'},\n        {'url': 'http://example.com/seg2'},\n    ]}\n    err = _http_error()\n    fd = make_fd(monkeypatch, params, download_results=[(True, b'f1'), err])\n    result = fd.real_download('file.mp4', info_dict)\n    assert result is True\n    assert fd.calls['download'] == ['http://example.com/seg1', 'http://example.com/seg2']\n    assert fd.calls['skip'] == [2]\n    assert fd.calls['error'] == []\n    assert len(fd.calls['finish']) == 1\n```\n\nTrace: frag 1: success. frag 2 (i=1, skip_unavailable=True \u2192 fatal=False): count=0, loop 0<=0 \u2192 HTTPError \u2192 count=1 \u2192 1<=0 False \u2192 no retry report. Loop 1<=0 False \u2192 exit. count(1) > 0 \u2192 not fatal \u2192 report_skip_fragment(2) \u2192 continue. Loop ends. finish. return True. \n\n**Test 8: DownloadError, not fatal \u2192 skip, break** (covers 63-68)\n\n```python\ndef test_download_error_nonfatal_skips(monkeypatch):\n    params = {}\n    info_dict = {'fragments': [\n        {'url': 'http://example.com/seg1'},\n        {'url': 'http://example.com/seg2'},\n    ]}\n    fd = make_fd(monkeypatch, params, download_results=[(True, b'f1'), DownloadError('boom')])\n    result = fd.real_download('file.mp4', info_dict)\n    assert result is True\n    assert fd.calls['skip'] == [2]\n    assert len(fd.calls['download']) == 2\n    assert len(fd.calls['finish']) == 1\n```\n\nTrace: frag 2 (i=1, fatal=False): DownloadError \u2192 not fatal \u2192 report_skip_fragment(2) \u2192 break. count is 0 \u2192 0 > 0 False \u2192 continue loop. finish. True. \n\n**Test 9: DownloadError, fatal \u2192 raises** (covers line 69 raise)\n\n```python\ndef test_download_error_fatal_raises(monkeypatch):\n    params = {}\n    info_dict = {'fragments': [{'url': 'http://example.com/seg1'}]}\n    fd = make_fd(monkeypatch, params, download_results=[DownloadError('boom')])\n    with pytest.raises(DownloadError):\n        fd.real_download('file.mp4', info_dict)\n    assert fd.calls['finish'] == []\n```\n\nTrace: i=0 \u2192 fatal=True. DownloadError \u2192 fatal \u2192 raise. Good.\n\nAlso test DownloadError with skip_unavailable_fragments=False on a later fragment \u2192 fatal \u2192 raise. That covers the `not skip_unavailable_fragments` part of line 40. Let me add:\n\n```python\ndef test_download_error_fatal_when_skip_disabled(monkeypatch):\n    params = {'skip_unavailable_fragments': False}\n    info_dict = {'fragments': [\n        {'url': 'http://example.com/seg1'},\n        {'url': 'http://example.com/seg2'},\n    ]}\n    fd = make_fd(monkeypatch, params, download_results=[(True, b'f1'), DownloadError('boom')])\n    with pytest.raises(DownloadError):\n        fd.real_download('file.mp4', info_dict)\n```\n\nThis covers line 40's `not skip_unavailable_fragments` \u2192 fatal=True for i=1, and line 69 raise.\n\n**Test 10: resume \u2014 fragments before fragment_index are skipped** (covers line 36-37 continue)\n\n```python\ndef test_resume_skips_already_downloaded_fragments(monkeypatch):\n    params = {}\n    info_dict = {'fragments': [\n        {'url': 'http://example.com/seg1'},\n        {'url': 'http://example.com/seg2'},\n        {'url': 'http://example.com/seg3'},\n    ]}\n    fd = make_fd(monkeypatch, params, fragment_index=2)\n    result = fd.real_download('file.mp4', info_dict)\n    assert result is True\n    assert fd.calls['download'] == ['http://example.com/seg3']\n```\n\nTrace: frag_index starts 0. i=0: frag_index=1 <= 2 \u2192 continue. i=1: frag_index=2 <= 2 \u2192 continue. i=2: frag_index=3 > 2 \u2192 download. Good. Covers continue branch.\n\nHmm wait, but there's a subtlety: for i=0 with continue, `fatal` is not computed... fine.\n\nAlso, note: with fragment_index=2 and 3 fragments, the first downloaded fragment is i=2, so fatal=True for it. Fine.\n\n**Test 11: assert fragment_base_url when no url and no base** \u2014 line 46 `assert fragment_base_url` fails \u2192 AssertionError. Should I test that? It's a branch (assert). Covering it: fragment without 'url' and without fragment_base_url \u2192 AssertionError propagates. Hmm, is that desirable to test? It improves branch coverage of line 46. Let me add:\n\n```python\ndef test_missing_base_url_asserts(monkeypatch):\n    params = {}\n    info_dict = {'fragments': [{'path': 'seg1.m4s'}]}\n    fd = make_fd(monkeypatch, params)\n    with pytest.raises(AssertionError):\n        fd.real_download('file.mp4', info_dict)\n```\n\nHmm, but wait \u2014 AssertionError... the assert statement with no message. `assert fragment_base_url` \u2192 if fragment_base_url is None \u2192 AssertionError. Yes. This covers the False branch of line 46. Good for branch coverage.\n\nHmm, but is this a \"correct\" test? It verifies the documented behavior (assert). It's a bit odd but it does improve coverage. I'll include it.\n\nNow, what about `total_frags` and ctx contents? I can assert ctx passed to prepare has filename and total_frags. Let me add assertions in test 1:\n\n```python\n    ctx = fd.calls['prepare'][0]\n    assert ctx['filename'] == 'file.mp4'\n    assert ctx['total_frags'] == 2\n```\n\nAnd in test 2 (test=True): total_frags == 1.\n\nAlso, `_download_fragment` receives (ctx, url, info_dict) \u2014 I could assert info_dict passed. Minor.\n\nNow \u2014 one important consideration: does `DashSegmentsFD.__init__` exist or does it use FileDownloader's? FileDownloader.__init__(self, ydl, params). Let me verify via get_info.\n\nAlso `FD_NAME` \u2014 DashSegmentsFD has `FD_NAME = 'dashsegments'`. Not needed for tests since we mock reporting.\n\nWait, one more thing: `real_download` signature \u2014 does it take (self, filename, info_dict)? Yes per the code.\n\nAnother consideration: `self.params.get('test', False)` \u2014 params is the dict passed in. Good.\n\nNow, about the HTTPError construction \u2014 let me double-check `compat_urllib_error.HTTPError` in youtube_dl.compat: `compat_urllib_error = urllib.error` (py3). HTTPError(url, code, msg, hdrs, fp). With hdrs={} and fp=io.BytesIO(b''):\n\nPython 3.8 urllib/response.py:\n\n```python\nclass addinfourl(addbase):\n    def __init__(self, fp, headers, url, code=None, msg=None):\n        if code is None:\n            code = msg\n        if msg is None:\n            msg = code\n        if headers is None:\n            headers = email.message.Message()\n        self.headers = headers\n        self.file = fp\n        self.url = url\n        self.code = code\n        addbase.__init__(self, fp)\n```\n\nHmm wait, in Python 3.9+:\n\n```python\nclass addinfourl(tempfile._TemporaryFileWrapper):\n    def __init__(self, fp, headers, url, code=None, msg=None):\n        if code is None:\n            code = msg\n        if msg is None:\n            msg = code\n        if headers is None:\n            headers = email.message.Message()\n        self.headers = headers\n        self.url = url\n        self.code = code\n        super().__init__(fp, \"<urllib response>\", unlink=None)\n```\n\nHmm, actually I think in 3.9 it's still addbase. In 3.11:\n\n```python\nclass addinfourl(tempfile._TemporaryFileWrapper):\n    def __init__(self, fp, headers, url, code=None, msg=None):\n        if code is None:\n            code = msg\n        if msg is None:\n            msg = code\n        if headers is None:\n            headers = email.message.Message()\n        self.headers = headers\n        self.url = url\n        self.code = code\n        super().__init__(fp, \"<urllib response>\", delete=False)\n```\n\nHmm, whatever \u2014 with fp=io.BytesIO() and headers={}, it should construct fine in all versions. Actually wait, in 3.11 `tempfile._TemporaryFileWrapper.__init__(self, file, name, delete=True)` \u2014 name=\"<urllib response>\", delete=False. Fine.\n\nActually hold on, in Python 3.12, HTTPError.__init__:\n\n```python\nclass HTTPError(URLError, urllib.response.addinfourl):\n    \"\"\"Raised when HTTP connection or request fails.\"\"\"\n    def __init__(self, url, code, msg, hdrs, fp):\n        self.code = code\n        self.msg = msg\n        self.hdrs = hdrs\n        self.fp = fp\n        self.filename = url\n        if fp is not None:\n            self.fp = fp\n        urllib.response.addinfourl.__init__(self, fp, hdrs, url, code)\n```\n\nOK fine with our args.\n\nAlternatively, to be extra safe, I could avoid constructing HTTPError directly and instead mock `_download_fragment` to raise an HTTPError created the same way. It's the same thing. I'll go with the direct construction with io.BytesIO and {}.\n\nHmm, one more consideration: `compat_urllib_error.HTTPError` \u2014 in the except clause, `except compat_urllib_error.HTTPError as err`. Our raised error must be an instance of that class. Since we construct it from the same class, yes.\n\nBut wait \u2014 there's a subtlety with youtube_dl.compat: `compat_urllib_error` might be a synthesized module. In youtube_dl/compat.py:\n\n```python\ntry:\n    import urllib.error as compat_urllib_error\nexcept ImportError:  # Python 2\n    import urllib2 as compat_urllib_error\n```\n\nActually it's:\n\n```python\ntry