l `_download_fragment` with a mocked `ydl.urlopen` that raises HTTPError.\n\nOption 2 is more realistic but more complex. Option 1 is simpler. Let me think about which is better for coverage. Both cover lines 53-62 in dash.py. Option 1 is simpler and more robust.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The HTTPError needs to be `compat_urllib_error.HTTPError`. Constructing one: `compat_urllib_error.HTTPError(url, code, msg, hdrs, fp)`. We can create one with `compat_urllib_error.HTTPError('http://example.com', 404, 'Not Found', None, None)` \u2014 actually HTTPError's constructor: `URLError(url, code, msg, hdrs, fp)`. It should work with None fp... Actually in some Python versions, HTTPError.__init__ calls `addinfourl` stuff... Let me check: \n\n```python\nclass HTTPError(URLError, urllib.response.addinfourl):\n    def __init__(self, url, code, msg, hdrs, fp):\n        self.code = code\n        self.msg = msg\n        self.hdrs = hdrs\n        self.fp = fp\n        self.filename = url\n        if fp is not None:\n            self.fp = fp\n        urllib.response.addinfourl.__init__(self, fp, hdrs, url, code)\n```\n\nHmm, in Python 3, `addinfourl.__init__(self, fp, headers, url, code=None)`. If fp is None... Let me check: in Python 3.x, `addinfourl.__init__` does:\n\n```python\n    def __init__(self, fp, headers, url, code=None, msg=None):\n        if code is None:\n            code = msg\n        if msg is None:\n            msg = code\n        if headers is None:\n            headers = email.message.Message()\n        self.headers = headers\n        self.file = fp\n        self.url = url\n        self.code = code\n```\n\nHmm, actually in newer versions it might be different. In Python 3.9:\n\n```python\nclass addinfourl(tempfile._TemporaryFileWrapper):\n    def __init__(self, fp, headers, url, code=None, msg=None):\n        if code is None:\n            code = msg\n        if msg is None:\n            msg = code\n        if headers is None:\n            headers = email.message.Message()\n        self.headers = headers\n        self.url = url\n        self.code = code\n        super().__init__(fp, \"<urllib response>\", unlink=None)\n```\n\nHmm, that's Python 3.11-ish. Anyway, passing None for fp and hdrs might work or might fail. To be safe, I can create the HTTPError with dummy values:\n\n```python\nimport io\nerr = compat_urllib_error.HTTPError('http://example.com/frag', 404, 'Not Found', {}, io.BytesIO(b''))\n```\n\nActually, in youtube_dl's compat module, `compat_urllib_error` is the urllib.error module in py3 or urllib2 in py2. Since we're testing with modern Python, it's urllib.error.\n\nLet me be safe: `compat_urllib_error.HTTPError('url', 404, 'Not Found', None, None)` \u2014 hmm, addinfourl with fp=None... In Python 3, `tempfile._TemporaryFileWrapper.__init__(self, file, name, delete=True)` \u2014 passing None file might break on close but we won't close it. Actually `super().__init__(fp, \"<urllib response>\", unlink=None)` \u2014 _TemporaryFileWrapper stores file. It should be fine as long as we don't call methods on it.\n\nActually, simpler: many tests in youtube-dl's test suite do:\n\n```python\nerr = compat_urllib_error.HTTPError('test', 404, 'not found', io.BytesIO(), io.BytesIO())\n```\n\nHmm wait, hdrs as io.BytesIO? That's weird but works since hdrs is just stored. Let me just use `compat_urllib_error.HTTPError('test', 404, 'not found', None, None)` and see. Actually, to be safest, let me check what happens in the version of Python being used. I can't run code, so let me be conservative:\n\n```python\nimport io\ndef make_http_error():\n    return compat_urllib_error.HTTPError(\n        'http://example.com/', 404, 'Not Found', {}, io.BytesIO(b''))\n```\n\nWith hdrs={} and fp=io.BytesIO(b''). In Python 3, addinfourl.__init__ with headers={} \u2014 it does `self.headers = headers`... wait in newer Python:\n\n```python\nclass addinfourl(tempfile._TemporaryFileWrapper):\n    def __init__(self, fp, headers, url, code=None, msg=None):\n        if code is None:\n            code = msg\n        if msg is None:\n            msg = code\n        if headers is None:\n            headers = email.message.Message()\n        self.headers = headers\n        self.url = url\n        self.code = code\n        super().__init__(fp, \"<urllib response>\", unlink=None)\n```\n\nHmm wait, actually I recall in Python 3.6-3.8:\n\n```python\nclass addinfourl(addbase):\n    def __init__(self, fp, headers, url, code=None, msg=None):\n        if code is None:\n            code = msg\n        if msg is None:\n            msg = code\n        if headers is None:\n            headers = email.message.Message()\n        self.headers = headers\n        self.file = fp\n        self.url = url\n        self.code = code\n        addbase.__init__(self, fp)\n```\n\nOK so with fp=io.BytesIO() and headers={}, it should work fine. Good.\n\nNow, HTTPError is also a subclass of URLError which is a subclass of OSError. And `DownloadError` \u2014 let me check: `class DownloadError(YoutubeDLError): def __init__(self, msg, exc_info=None)`. YoutubeDLError is `class YoutubeDLError(Exception)`. So DownloadError is an Exception, not OSError. Good, so HTTPError and DownloadError are distinct.\n\nNow let me think about the structure of the tests.\n\nThe DashSegmentsFD.real_download flow:\n\n```python\ndef real_download(self, filename, info_dict):\n    fragment_base_url = info_dict.get('fragment_base_url')\n    fragments = info_dict['fragments'][:1] if self.params.get('test', False) else info_dict['fragments']\n\n    ctx = {\n        'filename': filename,\n        'total_frags': len(fragments),\n    }\n\n    self._prepare_and_start_frag_download(ctx)\n\n    fragment_retries = self.params.get('fragment_retries', 0)\n    skip_unavailable_fragments = self.params.get('skip_unavailable_fragments', True)\n\n    frag_index = 0\n    for i, fragment in enumerate(fragments):\n        frag_index += 1\n        if frag_index <= ctx['fragment_index']:\n            continue\n        fatal = i == 0 or not skip_unavailable_fragments\n        count = 0\n        while count <= fragment_retries:\n            try:\n                fragment_url = fragment.get('url')\n                if not fragment_url:\n                    assert fragment_base_url\n                    fragment_url = urljoin(fragment_base_url, fragment['path'])\n                success, frag_content = self._download_fragment(ctx, fragment_url, info_dict)\n                if not success:\n                    return False\n                self._append_fragment(ctx, frag_content)\n                break\n            except compat_urllib_error.HTTPError as err:\n                count += 1\n                if count <= fragment_retries:\n                    self.report_retry_fragment(err, frag_index, count, fragment_retries)\n            except DownloadError:\n                if not fatal:\n                    self.report_skip_fragment(frag_index)\n                    break\n                raise\n\n        if count > fragment_retries:\n            if not fatal:\n                self.report_skip_fragment(frag_index)\n                continue\n            self.report_error('giving up after %s fragment retries' % fragment_retries)\n            return False\n\n    self._finish_frag_download(ctx)\n\n    return True\n```\n\nWait, there's a subtle bug-ish thing: if `fragment_retries` is 0 and an HTTPError occurs, count becomes 1, `count <= fragment_retries` is False so no retry report, then loop condition `count <= fragment_retries` \u2192 1 <= 0 is False, exit loop. Then `count > fragment_retries` \u2192 1 > 0 True \u2192 skip or error. OK.\n\nNow, the branches to cover:\n\nLine 20-21: `info_dict['fragments'][:1] if self.params.get('test', False) else info_dict['fragments']` \u2014 both branches.\n\nLine 34-37: loop; `if frag_index <= ctx['fragment_index']: continue` \u2014 both branches (continue and not continue).\n\nLine 40: `fatal = i == 0 or not skip_unavailable_fragments` \u2014 combinations: (i==0, skip=True) \u2192 fatal=True; (i>0, skip=True) \u2192 fatal=False; (i>0, skip=False) \u2192 fatal=True; (i==0, skip=False) \u2192 fatal=True.\n\nLine 44-47: `fragment.get('url')` truthy vs falsy \u2192 urljoin branch with assert fragment_base_url.\n\nLine 48-52: success True \u2192 append, break; success False \u2192 return False.\n\nLine 53-62: HTTPError with count <= retries \u2192 report_retry_fragment; HTTPError with count > retries \u2192 no report.\n\nLine 63-69: DownloadError with not fatal \u2192 skip, break; DownloadError with fatal \u2192 raise.\n\nLine 71-76: count > retries with not fatal \u2192 skip, continue; with fatal \u2192 report_error, return False. Also count <= retries (success) \u2192 proceed.\n\nLine 78-80: finish, return True.\n\nNow, how does ctx['fragment_index'] get set? In the real FragmentFD._prepare_frag_download:\n\nLet me recall youtube_dl/downloader/fragment.py:\n\n```python\n    def _prepare_and_start_frag_download(self, ctx):\n        self._prepare_frag_download(ctx)\n        self._start_frag_download(ctx)\n\n    def _prepare_frag_download(self, ctx):\n        if 'ctx' in self.__dict__:\n            raise Exception('Already exists')\n        ctx = self.ctx = ctx.copy()\n        ctx['fragment_index'] = 0\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, I'm not 100% sure. Let me use get_info to check `_prepare_and_start_frag_download`, `_download_fragment`, `_append_fragment`, `_finish_frag_download`, `report_retry_fragment`, `report_skip_fragment`, `report_error`, and the classes.\n\nActually, for the resume test (line 36-37 continue branch), I need ctx['fragment_index'] > 0 after `_prepare_and_start_frag_download`. If the real implementation sets it to 0 always, then to test the continue branch I'd need to mock `_prepare_and_start_frag_download` to set fragment_index to e.g. 1, with 2 fragments. Then frag 1 is skipped (continue), frag 2 is downloaded.\n\nAlternatively, I mock `_prepare_and_start_frag_download` in