that would patch the class globally; instance-level is cleaner and monkeypatch handles restore.\n\nHmm, actually there's a subtlety: monkeypatch.setattr(obj, name, value) requires the attribute to exist (unless raising=False). Inherited attributes exist via the class, so `getattr(fd, '_download_fragment')` works and monkeypatch will set instance attribute and on teardown `delattr(fd, name)`... Actually monkeypatch records (obj, name, old_value) and on undo does setattr(obj, name, old_value) \u2014 for an inherited attribute, old_value is the bound method... hmm, setattr(fd, '_download_fragment', bound_method) would set an instance attribute equal to the bound method \u2014 that's harmless (it would shadow with the same bound method). Actually monkeypatch is smarter: for module attributes it deletes if it didn't exist... Let me recall: monkeypatch.setattr(target, name, value) \u2014 on undo, it does `setattr(target, name, oldval)` if oldval is not a \"didn't exist\" marker, else delattr. Since the attribute exists (via class), oldval = bound method, and undo does setattr(fd, '_download_fragment', <bound method>) \u2014 sets instance attr. Slightly redundant but harmless, and the fd object is discarded anyway. Fine.\n\nActually, simpler and equally clean: since each test creates its own fd, plain assignment `fd._download_fragment = fake` has no pollution risk. But the prompt explicitly asks to use monkeypatch/pytest-mock where appropriate. I'll use monkeypatch for the instance patches \u2014 it's fine.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually there might be an issue with monkeypatch.setattr on instance for methods: `monkeypatch.setattr(fd, '_prepare_and_start_frag_download', fake_prepare)` \u2014 this works.\n\nNow, about creating the fd: `DashSegmentsFD(ydl, params)`. FileDownloader.__init__:\n\n```python\n    def __init__(self, ydl, params):\n        \"\"\"Constructor. Takes a YoutubeDL object and a dictionary of\n        options.\"\"\"\n        self.ydl = ydl\n        self._progress