akes. Let me do a reasonable batch.\n\nActually, since all these calls are independent, I can batch them all in one block. Let me do that.\n\nAfter getting info, I'll write the final test file.\n\nLet me also think about whether `real_download` might call `self.to_screen` etc. via the mocked methods \u2014 no, all reporting goes through the methods we mock or through ydl (MagicMock). Actually `report_retry_fragment` real impl calls `self.to_screen(...)` \u2014 FileDownloader.to_screen calls `self.ydl.to_screen(...)`. With MagicMock ydl, fine. But we mock it anyway.\n\nOne more thing: `DownloadError('boom')` \u2014 DownloadError.__init__(self, msg, exc_info=None): stores msg. Raising it is fine.\n\nNow let me also double check the `while count <= fragment_retries` loop for the DownloadError-not-fatal case: after break, count is still 0 (not incremented), so `count > fragment_retries` \u2192 0 > 0 \u2192 False \u2192 proceed to next fragment. Good.\n\nAnd for DownloadError fatal: raise propagates out of real_download. Good.\n\nNow, about the `assert fragment_base_url` test \u2014 AssertionError. But wait: could the AssertionError be caught by anything? No, only HTTPError and DownloadError are caught. So it propagates. Good.\n\nHmm, one thing to double check: in test 3, `urljoin('http://example.com/base/', 'seg1.m4s')`. urljoin is compat_urlparse.urljoin. Result: 'http://example.com/base/seg1.m4s'. Yes.\n\nBut what if fragment_base_url doesn't end with '/'? e.g. 'http://example.com/base' + 'seg1.m4s' \u2192 'http://example.com/seg1.m4s'. I'll use trailing slash to be predictable.\n\nNow let me also consider: should tests use the real `_prepare_and_start_frag_download`? If I don't mock it, the real one runs `_prepare_frag_download` which does:\n\n```python\n    def _prepare_frag_download(self, ctx):\n        if 'ctx' in self.__dict__:\n            raise Exception('Already exists')\n        ctx = self.ctx = ctx.copy()\n        ctx['dest'] = ctx['filename'] + '.part'  # h